# Methods (mental model)

Methods is a universal mental model — a conceptual tool that helps understand and navigate complex situations by providing frameworks for recognizing patterns and relationships. It functions as a meta-layer above specific techniques, offering a lens for evaluating any structured approach to complexity.

## Definition

Methods provides a mental shortcut or framework for making sense of patterns and relationships in the world. Rather than prescribing a single technique, it encompasses the broader principle that structured approaches to information, attention, and action help manage complexity.

## Cross-Source Perspectives

Three established sources support this model:

- [[Getting Things Done - David Allen]] emphasizes structured methods for managing attention and action through capture, organization, and review cycles.
- [[How to Take Smart Notes - Sönke Ahrens]] provides a methodological framework for knowledge work through incremental note-taking and progressive summarization.
- [[Stillness Is the Key - Ryan Holiday]] advocates for stillness and attentional methods as tools for clarity and decision-making.

## Relationship to Other Mental Models

Methods sits alongside [[recipes-mental-model]] and [[framework-template]] as a meta-framework. While [[recipes-mental-model]] focuses on structured, deconstructable frameworks and [[problem.md]] addresses problem-solving as a universal model, Methods abstracts further — it is the principle that structured approaches themselves are valuable navigational tools.

## Open Questions

The boundary between Methods and existing entries like [[recipes-mental-model]] or [[framework-template]] is unclear. Further development is needed to distinguish when a specific technique counts as a "method" versus when it falls under a more granular category.

## Confidence

Moderate (0.7) — the model is well-supported by source references but remains abstract and underdeveloped.

